# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-master.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: berkeley-master.logic.r3 has nothing from it; berkeley-master.logic.r3.2 has "frame_ideas_omit"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; berkeley-master.logic.r3.3 has "imagine_unperceived"
berkeley-master.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps berkeley-master.logic.r3.2, to: Suppose "you can imagine trees in a park or books in a closet with nobody by to perceive them" is true and "this imagining is no more than framing ideas you call books and trees while omitting to frame the idea of anyone who may perceive them" is false. Is S2, "I answer, you may so, there is no difficulty in it; but what is all this, I beseech you, more than framing in your mind certain ideas which you call BOOKS and TREES, and the same time omitting to frame the idea of any one that may perceive them?", true then?
berkeley-master.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley-master.logic.r3 and berkeley-master.logic.r3.3 have "imagining_shows → conceive_without_mind"; berkeley-master.logic.r3.2 has "frame_ideas_omit ∧ you_perceive → ¬imagining_shows"; they differ when only "this imagining is no more than framing ideas you call books and trees while omitting to frame the idea of anyone who may perceive them", "you yourself perceive or think of the trees and books all the while", "the imagining shows that you can conceive it possible that the objects of your thought exist without the mind" and "you can conceive it possible that the objects of your thought exist without the mind" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-master.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-master.logic.r3 and berkeley-master.logic.r3.3 have "¬imagining_shows"; berkeley-master.logic.r3.2 has "¬conceive_without_mind"; they differ when only "you can conceive it possible that the objects of your thought exist without the mind" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-master.logic.r3: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: berkeley-master.logic.r3 and berkeley-master.logic.r3.3 put it at S4, "This therefore is nothing to the purpose; it only shows you have the power of imagining or forming ideas in your mind: but it does not show that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind."; berkeley-master.logic.r3.2 at S5, "To make out this, IT IS NECESSARY THAT YOU CONCEIVE THEM EXISTING UNCONCEIVED OR UNTHOUGHT OF, WHICH IS A MANIFEST REPUGNANCY."
berkeley-master.logic.r3: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-master.logic.r3 and berkeley-master.logic.r3.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "This therefore is nothing to the purpose; it only shows you have the power of imagining or forming ideas in your mind: but it does not show that you can conceive it possible the objects of your thought may exist without the mind.", rather than S5, "To make out this, IT IS NECESSARY THAT YOU CONCEIVE THEM EXISTING UNCONCEIVED OR UNTHOUGHT OF, WHICH IS A MANIFEST REPUGNANCY."?
